<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marduk-zakir-shumi I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 855–819 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marduk-zakir-shumi I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from about 855 to 819 BCE. When his brother Marduk-bel-usate revolted, he called on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, who defeated the rebel in 850 BCE; the two kings are shown clasping hands on a throne base from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a>. He later supported Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> V of Assyria in his own succession war, and a treaty between the two survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marduk-zakir-shumi-i.jpg" alt="Marduk-zakir-shumi I" width="320" height="631" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marduk-zakir-shumi_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Marduk-zâkir-šumi, whose name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> pronounced the name, ruled in the mixed dynastic period known in antiquity as the dynasty of E, and was a contemporary and ally of the Assyrian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> and Šamši-Adad V. Few inscriptions of his reign survive. A kudurru of his second year grants land to Ibni-Ištar, a priest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eanna">Eanna</a> temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, and names as witnesses the crown prince Marduk-balāssu-iqbi and Nazi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, the first holder of that office since Kassite times. A lapis lazuli seal of his shows Marduk's statue resting on the dragon Mušḫuššu.</p><p>His younger brother Marduk-bēl-ušati rebelled and set up a brief regime in the Diyāla region, seizing Daban. In 851 and 850 BCE Shalmaneser III came to the king's aid. In the first campaign the rebel was beaten outside Ganannate but held the city; in the second it fell, and he fled with some of his officers to Arman, which was taken after a siege. Shalmaneser's Black Obelisk says he cut down the rebel and his officers with the sword. The Assyrian king then received tribute from Chaldean leaders, the earliest record of these groups, and made sacrifices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a> and Kutha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The king repaid the favour about 826 BCE, when Shalmaneser's son Aššur-danin-apli rebelled and 27 cities went over to him. A treaty between Šamši-Adad V and Marduk-zâkir-šumi seems to place the Assyrian in an inferior position, and it ends with curses apparently copied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>, leaving out the god Aššur. It may have been sealed by the marriage of the king's daughter Shammuramat, the inspiration for the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semiramis">Semiramis</a>, to Šamši-Adad. Šamši-Adad resented his subordinate place and later took a terrible revenge in the reign of Marduk-balāssu-iqbi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marduk-zakir-shumi I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Marduk-zakir-shumi I</b> relied on Shalmaneser III of the Assyrian Empire to put down his brother's revolt, a sign of growing dependence.</blockquote><a href="/state/dynasty-of-e">Dynasty of E</a></li></ul></section>
